Top-down interior map of a small artisan frame-maker's shop. D&D style, warm tones, no grid (illustration/theater of the mind, not battle map). Approximately 20x15 feet.

LAYOUT:
- SOUTH: entrance door (wooden, glass pane on top half) opening onto a narrow alley. A small bell hangs above the door.
- WEST WALL: frames of every size hanging on the wall — gilded, plain wood, ornate, simple. Some stacked on the floor leaning against the wall.
- NORTH: workbench (sturdy wood table) with tools: small saws, chisels, clamps, pots of glue, gold leaf sheets, brushes. A stool behind it.
- EAST WALL: shelves with finished frames, rolls of canvas, jars of varnish and pigments. A curtain (faded red fabric) hides a back door leading to a small storage room.
- CENTER: open floor space with wood shavings scattered. A single chair for customers.

DETAILS:
- Light enters from a high narrow window (north-east corner), casting a diagonal beam across the workbench.
- Smell suggested by visual cues: wood shavings on floor, open glue pot, dried herbs hanging near the window (to mask chemical smells).
- Walls: plastered, cream-colored, stained with age. One wall has faint rectangular marks where paintings once hung (removed long ago).
- A small drawer unit behind the workbench — one drawer slightly open (where Sberluccica keeps the old letter).
- Outside the shop window: the faded gold sign reading "CORNICI E RESTAURI — M. C. SBERLUCCICA" visible in reverse.

MOOD: Quiet, modest, slightly melancholic. A place where an artist became a craftsman. Orderly but not prosperous.

STYLE: Top-down illustration, D&D fantasy art style, warm earth tones (honey wood, cream walls, faded gold). Soft lighting. No characters. No grid lines.

SIZE: 20x15 feet (4x3 squares equivalent, but no grid drawn).
